From: Christina M Grimes <cg39+@andrew.cmu.edu>
Subject: Inventions & concepts

I had been confused about the origins of the invention--money--
language--concepts thread, until I scrolled back and found I had missed
Tim Starr's Sept. 10 post, that reads in part:

%(Quoting me):
%>You can draw a kind of parallel with concepts. Concepts are a human
%>invention, that is, the invention of intelligent, rational creatures.
%>They are not "objective" only in the sense that we did not discover
%>them, but invented them: similarly, we did not discover rights, but we
%>invented them.
%
%Not only are concepts most certainly not any kind of invention
%whatsoever, neither are rights.  To dispose of the claim that concepts 
%are made, rather than found, requires nothing more than to consider the
%question: how can anyone invent anything without already having any 
%concepts?  They can't. This is the fallacy of self-exclusion, also
known %as the problem of self-referential refutation.  (Most of the worst
%fallacies of today are of this kind.)
%
%Both concepts and rights are most certainly discoveries, not inventions.

This may be a little late, but...

I accept your point, to a certain degree. However, I think there is a
distinction we both missed: concepts and rights are neither discoveries
nor inventions, but in between, comparable to spontaneous orders. You
are right that we do not devise concepts (or rights) the same way we
might devise or "invent" a machine, but neither do we discover them as
we do the structure of a tree. Discovery implies objectivity, that the
thing being discovered exists whether we take notice of it or not, and
that the thing discovered is not affected by the action. Invention
implies conscious effort to create something that did not exist before,
and the invention, while being internally consistent and consistent with
discoveries, is arbitrary (i.e., there are other, equally valid ways of
causing the inventions's effect.) 

Concepts are "developed": they are created, like inventions, but are
objective, like discoveries. Concepts do not exist unless there are
intelligent beings to have and understand concepts. At the same time,
concepts are not arbitrary--they depend entirely on reality and
objectivity.

I did not pay enough attention to this distinction, but this idea is
somewhat implicit in my excerpt above.

Also, I see no reason to conclude that invention can't go on without
concepts. What about the monkey in those experiments that piles boxes
and stands on them in order to reach a banana suspended from the
ceiling? I would think it had invented a kind of stool. It certainly has
no idea of what a "stool" is, or what a thing that elevates is, but it
just stacked those boxes to have something higher to stand on. Even
though this is sticky ground (we need to know just what's an invention
and what's a tool), I just can't think of any better example right now.
{:)

-> Primarily, rights are not the result of human design, just like money,
-> language, and many other social formations.  They result from human
-> action, but not from human design.
-> 
-> The notion that these things are made, rather than found, is just as
-> creationist as the notion that the Universe and everything in it are 
-> the Creation of some Supreme Being.  There is no such Supreme Being, 
-> Divine or secular.

(You can tell I'm playing with my new quoter program.)

That first paragraph I concur with in full, as it's entirely consistent
with the distinction just made. I also concur that creationism (and its
implications, i.e. supreme beings) is wrong. However, I don't see how
stating that rights and concepts are dependent on the humans that
develop them is creationist. Although you can not devise a new concept,
but have to form it around objective things or ideas, if there were no
intelligent beings to form any concepts, the concepts would not exist.
Their *potential* existed, but until an intelligent being first formed a
concept, there was no such thing. They are dependent on the objective
world, but first dependent on us for forming them in the first place.

Therefore, if we define rights as dependent on the holder's ability (and
desire) to grasp and reciprocate them--i.e., as applicable only to
intelligent beings (which we seem to have agreed on)--then, like
concepts, they did not exist before some intelligent beings first
implemented them. Not only that, but "rights" themselves are concepts,
that had to first be conceived before they could exist.

-> Renaissance Now!
-> Think Universally, Act Selfishly

I agree.

Ratha

