From: Alexander Chislenko <sasha@cs.umb.edu>
Subject: $:How to save it on postage,not as boring as it sounds

    The postal service takes a lot more money from me for delivering
my letters than this service should cost (I send them either
locally, in Cambridge, or to a few big cities like LA and NY).
It tells me that it needs more of my money to support the same postal
price in other regions of the country, for its own and other customers'
convenience.  I am not allowed to entrust my first-class letters to any
other carrier. (Fedex, UPS and Checkfree provide different services).
    This looks more like an extortion to me than a free contract.
 
    Same thing with taxes: the government wants me to pay more money than
I think its service is worth, for things that *it* considers useful,
convinient or important. Frankly, I do consider some things that government
does useful; I am getting 'more than nothing' - just as in the case with the
postal service.  The problem is though that this tax service is bad, slow
and overpriced; extra money is taken to support people and goals that I do
not want to support; I have never chosen this service freely, or have been
offered an alternative.  
   Just like the postal office.  And very much unlike MacDonalds.
 
   Also, by the same logic, being in an implicit contract with the financial
system that provides me with money mass to be used to represent my earned
posessions, I should return any coin that I pick on the sidewalk to the
Federal Reserve (or at least not use it). 
   Using a quarter you found allows you to *steal* a part of the national
economic pie that you haven't earned - just like sending an unstamped
letter does.
   I should confess, I have committed this crime many times in several
countries.
   
   I don't know how righteous we want to get.
 
   I considered this trick with an unstamped letter just funny.  
   
   Since this way of sending letters is probably no more reliable than it
is legal, the only situation I can imagine a reasonable person using it in,
is one of despair and urgency, in which case it should be worth it.
 
   In any case, I don't think we should insult each other over little things
like this.

/sasha

