Subject: The Unbearable Argumentativeness of Perry :-)

Perry writes:
>You've just basically pretended that they never argued against this
>notion by defining the problem out of existance. However, the
>Cartesian argument that you can't be sure of your senses is exactly
>what Rand claimed was false, and VR systems show that she was just
>plain dead wrong -- doesn't matter how often you deny it, Tim. If you
>want to claim otherwise, go off to Kelley's book and find a quote that
>proves that I'm wrong. It shouldn't take you more than a few minutes
>assuming that the quote is actually there to be found.

I'm sorry, Perry, but I can't agree with your statement. The "VR argument"
is a simple reformulation of the Cartesian argument, not a proof of it.
The question of whether the proposed VR system could be built aside, what
you've done is substitute one "deceiver" for another, both of which are
strictly hypothetical animals.

Elias Israel
