Date: Wed, 15 Sep 93 7:37:59 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Landfill

It's probably possible to more than fund the cost of garbage
collection by treating fresh trash (as well as landfill) as
low-grade ore of various desirable substances.  While separation
of garbage isotopically will probably take a while yet to develop,
before there were statist recycling laws the "cream" [aluminum
cans] was already being mined* by independent entrepreneurs,
now out of work.  This suggests that more sophisticated "garbage
picking", either with extensive automation or in a large building
just across the Rio Grande, should be feasible as a first step.
Many barely broken consumer items end up in the trash; many of
them could be cost-effectively fixed; this means that we can
profitably open a technical school in Ciudad Juarez to train
electric-shaver repairpersons.

Besides saving the environment from strip-mining and exhaustion
of precious landfill space, we will be educating the poor!

As to the information (e.g. phone bills, home videos, ...) contained
in garbage, we wouldn't collect that!  Honest!

derek
Onward!  Upward!  Outward!  Into the garbage!

* '"cream" ... being mined' : this is creative use of mixed metaphor.

